  AssetFactory — Phase 3
    deployment
      √ deploys with correct admin address and an empty registry (574ms)
      √ rejects a zero admin (133ms)
    issuer authorisation
      √ admin can authorise an issuer (131ms)
      √ authorising an already-authorised issuer or the zero address reverts (651ms)
      √ admin can revoke an issuer (60ms)
      √ revoking an address that is not authorised reverts
      √ non-admin cannot authorise or revoke an issuer (76ms)
    asset creation
      √ authorised issuer can create an asset (deploys AssetToken + AssetSale) (221ms)
      √ created asset is recorded in the registry with full metadata and tokenToSale (199ms)
      √ issuer creates two different assets that are registered independently (221ms)
      √ unauthorised address, including the admin, cannot create an asset (79ms)
      √ createAsset with unitPriceWei == 0 reverts
      √ createAsset with totalSupply == 0 reverts
      √ getAsset reverts for an ID that was never assigned (197ms)
      √ rejects an out-of-range assetType
    revocation scope
      √ revoked issuer cannot create new assets but existing contracts keep operating (358ms)
    issuer isolation
      √ issuer A cannot whitelist investors on issuer B's token (59ms)
      √ issuer A cannot allocate, withdraw, close or recover on issuer B's sale (77ms)

  AssetSale — Phase 3
    deployment
      √ deploys with correct assetToken, pricePerToken, and issuer (205ms)
      √ rejects a zero token, zero issuer, or zero price (111ms)
      √ exposes no function to change the price after creation
    allocation
      √ issuer can allocate tokens (whitelist sale, approve, allocate) (103ms)
      √ allocate reverts until the issuer whitelists the sale contract
      √ allocate without an approval reverts
      √ non-issuer cannot allocate (283ms)
      √ allocate with insufficient issuer balance reverts
      √ allocate of zero reverts
    buying (instant subscription flow)
      √ whitelisted investor buys 100 tokens with exact ETH payment (103ms)
      √ investor underpays: reverts
      √ investor overpays: reverts
      √ unapproved investor attempts purchase: reverts
      √ revoked investor cannot buy
      √ investor buys more than inventory: reverts
      √ buying the entire inventory succeeds and leaves nothing for sale (62ms)
      √ buy of zero quantity reverts
      √ rejects plain ETH sent outside buyTokens
    proceeds
      √ issuer can withdraw ETH proceeds after a purchase (297ms)
      √ non-issuer withdraws proceeds: reverts
      √ withdrawProceeds with zero balance reverts
      √ invariant 8: a re-entrant contract issuer is paid exactly once and the balance is zero (358ms)
      √ a failed payout reverts and leaves the proceeds claimable (65ms)
    closing and inventory recovery
      √ issuer can close the sale, after which buying and allocating revert (207ms)
      √ recoverUnsoldTokens reverts while the sale is open
      √ issuer recovers all unsold inventory after closing and can still withdraw proceeds (126ms)
      √ non-issuer cannot close the sale or recover inventory
    invariants
      √ invariant 3: sale ETH balance equals tokens sold times pricePerToken minus withdrawals (187ms)
      √ invariant 2: supply equals the sum of balances, and holders are eligible absent revocation (276ms)
    Phase 3 lifecycle demonstration
      √ create 10,000 CPT, whitelist sale, deposit 5,000, investor pays 0.1 ETH for 100 CPT (47ms)

  AssetToken AUD-004 regression
    √ rejects a transfer to an unapproved investor without changing balances (97ms)

  AssetToken — Phase 2
    A — deployment and fixed supply
      √ sets the name, symbol, and whole-unit decimals (66ms)
      √ mints exactly 10,000 whole units to the designated issuer
      √ emits the standard mint Transfer from the zero address
      √ whitelists the issuer and leaves other addresses, including zero, ineligible
      √ grants the issuer the administrator and compliance roles
      √ rejects a zero issuer
      √ rejects a zero supply (41ms)
      √ exposes no mint or burn entry point, even to the administrator
      √ assigns supply and roles to the issuer instead of a different deploying account (73ms)
      √ supports construction by a contract without granting that contract authority (95ms)
    B — whitelist and role administration
      √ approves investors B and C with publicly readable status
      √ emits investor, status, and operator on approval and revocation (44ms)
      √ rejects unauthorised whitelist update approved=true
      √ rejects a zero investor when approved=true
      √ rejects unauthorised whitelist update approved=false (96ms)
      √ rejects a zero investor when approved=false
      √ treats repeated status updates as no-ops without duplicate events (74ms)
      √ revokes a funded investor without confiscation, burning, or Transfer events (116ms)
      √ delegates compliance and records the actual delegate as operator (44ms)
      √ prevents unauthorised accounts from granting themselves compliance authority
      √ prevents compliance-only delegates from granting roles (38ms)
      √ removes compliance authority immediately when the role is revoked
      √ requires compliance authority even for an administrator whose compliance role was revoked (62ms)
    C — direct transfers and revocation
      √ transfers 100 units to B and emits the standard Transfer event (69ms)
      √ rejects an unapproved recipient and preserves balances and supply (468ms)
      √ allows B to transfer 20 units to approved C (44ms)
      √ rejects a revoked sender even when the recipient is approved (42ms)
      √ rejects transfers into a revoked holder with a positive balance (48ms)
      √ rejects transfers when both holder and recipient are ineligible (47ms)
      √ restores sending and receiving after reapproval (81ms)
      √ applies the same eligibility requirements to the issuer (65ms)
    D — approvals and delegated transfers
      √ creates and replaces allowances with standard Approval events (53ms)
      √ allows an unwhitelisted spender to transfer between approved holders (77ms)
      √ rolls back balances and allowance for an ineligible delegated recipient (122ms)
      √ checks the actual holder and rolls back allowance when that holder is revoked (162ms)
      √ preserves the standard insufficient-allowance error and unchanged state
      √ preserves approvals and approval revocation for ineligible wallets (126ms)
      √ preserves unlimited allowances across successful transfers (52ms)
      √ rolls back allowance spending when the delegated holder has insufficient balance (57ms)
    E — edge cases, contract holders, and instance isolation
      √ rejects a direct transfer exceeding the balance
      √ permits a zero-value transfer between eligible accounts and emits Transfer
      √ enforces both eligibility checks even for zero-value transfers
      √ allows zero-value delegated transfers but still checks holder eligibility (76ms)
      √ preserves standard zero-recipient errors for direct and delegated transfers (54ms)
      √ preserves standard errors for a zero spender or a zero delegated owner
      √ supports self-transfers without altering balance or supply
      √ allows an approved smart-contract holder to receive and forward inventory (157ms)
      √ isolates roles, whitelist, allowances, balances, and supply across assets (352ms)
      √ conserves supply across deterministic transfers, revocation, and failures (231ms)
    Local demonstration
      √ demonstrates CPT issuance, unapproved D rejection, and final balances 9,900 / 80 / 20 (124ms)

  Phase 3 audit-1 regressions
    AUD-005 invariant 1 through a revocation
      √ a revoked holder keeps its balance but cannot send, receive or buy until re-approved (313ms)


